In C++20 multithreading, memory ordering specifies how memory accesses are ordered around atomic operations without requiring heavy kernel mutex locks.

1. `std::memory_order_seq_cst` (Sequential Consistency - Default):
- Enforces a globally agreed-upon total execution order across all threads.
- Penalty: Incurs full memory barriers (fences) on hardware like ARM64 and x86, which can flush CPU instruction pipelines and stall cache coherence controllers.

2. `std::memory_order_acquire` and `std::memory_order_release` (Acquire-Release Semantics):
- Synchronizes pairwise between the releasing thread and acquiring thread.
- Release: No prior read/write operations in the current thread can be reordered after this store.
- Acquire: No subsequent read/write operations in the current thread can be reordered before this load.

Lock-Free Synchronization Example:
```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <thread>
#include <string>
#include <iostream>

std::string shared_payload;
std::atomic<bool> is_ready{false};

void producer_thread() {
    // Non-atomic payload assignment
    shared_payload = "Computed high-performance payload #42";

    // Release barrier: guarantees shared_payload is visible in cache
    // BEFORE is_ready becomes true.
    is_ready.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
}

void consumer_thread() {
    // Acquire barrier: ensures subsequent access to shared_payload
    // occurs strictly AFTER is_ready is observed as true.
    while (!is_ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
        #if defined(__aarch64__)
        asm volatile("yield"); // Power-friendly spin on Apple Silicon ARM64
        #endif
    }

    std::cout << "Received: " << shared_payload << "\n";
}

int main() {
    std::thread t2(consumer_thread);
    std::thread t1(producer_thread);
    t1.join();
    t2.join();
    return 0;
}
```
On Apple Silicon ARM64, `store(release)` compiles to `stlr` (Store-Release) and `load(acquire)` compiles to `ldar` (Load-Acquire), eliminating expensive full system barriers (`dmb ish`).
